// WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS — it is the counter-example that DECLINED a priced
// mechanism, and without it the decline is a sentence nobody can re-run.
//
// `recvmutbind` (borrow_check.cpp, check_recv_conflict) priced at CEILING 1 /
// COST 0 on the whole acceptance corpus: one ledger row
// (borrowck_many-mutable-borrows, E0596) for no refused legal program. COST 0
// is not a safety claim — a corpus refuses only programs it CONTAINS — and
// this program, written by hand, is the one it did not contain.
//
// `mut` written in a PATTERN is legal Rust and compiles here, but it never
// reaches the borrow checker's `is_mut_binding`: declare_pat_bindings calls
// declare_var, and the LIR pattern schema carries no by-value-`mut` key
// (IS_MUT is PatRefBind/PatRefPat only; BIND_MODES 0 means "by value" whether
// or not `mut` was written). Arming the probe refused this program with
// "cannot borrow 'v' as mutable: not declared as mut".
//
// ⚠ THE SAME OVER-REFUSAL IS ALREADY LANDED AT THE OTHER RECEIVER SPELLING:
// `match e { E::A(mut f) => { f.bump(); } }` over a STRUCT receiver, and
// `... => { let r: &mut Foo = &mut f; }`, are refused TODAY by the
// AddrOfTemp/AddrOf mut-binding arms. They are not landed as fail fixtures
// here because those refusals are WRONG, not because they are absent. The
// prerequisite for both — the row and the repair — is a per-binding
// by-value-`mut` flag parallel to BINDINGS, set by sema.
package bc_recvmutbind_pattern_mut_binding;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

enum E { A(Vec<i64>), B }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let e: E = E::A(Vec::<i64>::new());
    match e {
        E::A(mut v) => { v.push(1i64); }
        E::B => {}
    }
    return 0i32;
}
